window.docContents[189].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Bounded-Solution-Operators":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Elliptic Regularity and Solution Operators","header":"5.6.2. Bounded Solution Operators","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Bounded-Solution-Operators"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations":{"contents":"To state , we choose a domain, a function space, and a notion\nof derivative. A classical equation holds pointwise; a distributional equation\nholds against test functions. Boundary conditions impose further requirements.\nThe operators and solution predicates below record these choices explicitly.\n\nThe classical operators are built from the Fréchet derivative on Mathlib's\nEuclidean . Above them sit typed problem records that keep an\nequation, its data, and its solution concept apart, so that a pointwise residual\nidentity, a weak formulation, and Hadamard well-posedness are three propositions\nrather than three readings of one sentence.\nSolving a problem is itself a\ndefinition: it requires the field to satisfy every identity stored in the\nproblem record.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"3. Foundations","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations"},"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Radial-and-planar-differential-coordinates":{"contents":"In dimension , the radial expression is\n. It specializes to the familiar polar and spherical formulas in\ndimensions two and three. Real division is total in Lean, so the definition itself also has a\nvalue at zero; analytic theorems interpreting it as a coordinate Laplacian assume that the\nradius is positive or nonzero.By the radial Laplacian theorem,\nthis expression is the Cartesian Laplacian of  at\n, provided the profile is twice continuously differentiable at that radius.\n\nThe two profiles that matter for fundamental solutions are the logarithm in\ndimension two and powers of the radius in higher dimensions. For the logarithm\nthe two terms of the two-dimensional radial Laplacian cancel exactly.\n\nOn the positive radial branch,This one-variable identity underlies the two-dimensional logarithmic\nfundamental solution away from its singularity.\n\nDifferentiate on the positive half-line:  and\n. Substitution into the two-dimensional radial\nLaplacian gives .\n\nFor a power profile the radial Laplacian is again a power, with a coefficient\nquadratic in the exponent. Its zeros  and  identify the constant\nand the inverse-power harmonic profiles.\n\nAt every positive radius, the dimension- radial Laplacian sends the power profile  to\n. For , the choice  gives the harmonic\ninverse-power profile. In dimension two this choice gives only the constant\nprofile; the nonconstant logarithmic profile is covered by the preceding theorem.\n\nOn , the first two derivatives of  are\n and . Adding the radial correction\n and collecting coefficients yields\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","header":"3.3.1.2. Radial and planar differential coordinates","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Radial-and-planar-differential-coordinates"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Systems-and-Elementary-Waves":{"contents":"For a system , hyperbolicity concerns the eigenstructure of the\nJacobian . Genuine nonlinearity and linear degeneracy are additional\nconditions on characteristic fields. With the required regularity, linearly\ndegenerate integral curves give contact connections; rarefaction connections\nalso require increasing speed along the oriented curve. Rankine–Hugoniot\nrelations describe shocks.\n\nA local wave family supplies transformations with prescribed strict\nzero-strength derivatives; elementary-wave admissibility is a separate\nhypothesis. When the chosen characteristic vectors at the left state form\na basis, the composed wave map has invertible derivative. The inverse\nfunction theorem then gives a unique strength vector within a source\nneighborhood for each nearby right state. For a  strictly hyperbolic\nflux whose families are each genuinely nonlinear or linearly degenerate,\nlocal shock curves and integral curves supply such a family, and the\nstrengths assemble into a bounded self-similar weak solution of the Riemann\nproblem.\n\nEntropy pairs supply a scalar conservation or dissipation law compatible with\nthe system. For the p-system we verify strict hyperbolicity, mechanical\nentropy, Riemann invariants, and diagonal transport form, and construct a\nlocal Riemann solution satisfying the mechanical entropy inequality. For\n systems in Riemann coordinates, compressive data rule out a global\nclassical solution. For a smooth, strictly hyperbolic, genuinely nonlinear\nflux, small jumps admit viscous profiles exactly when they are Lax shocks.\nAlmost-everywhere limits of bounded viscous solutions are entropy solutions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.2.3. Systems and Elementary Waves","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Systems-and-Elementary-Waves"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Weak-Evolution-Foundations":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.5. Weak Evolution Foundations","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Weak-Evolution-Foundations"}});